 [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/157967"} "36 from 9 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "For decades, Great Britain had been the dominant force in the coxless fours, and had taken gold in the event at the last four editions of the Olympic Games. In Berlin, the nation was represented by the " [:a {:href "/affiliations/950"} "London Rowing Club"] " – " [:a {:href "/athletes/37207"} "Alan Barrett"] ", " [:a {:href "/athletes/37232"} "Martin Bristow"] ", " [:a {:href "/athletes/37337"} "Peter Jackson"] ", and " [:a {:href "/athletes/37481"} "Jan Sturrock"] " –, which had been the runner-up in the Stewards’ Challenge Cup (then the equivalent of a World Championship in the event) in 1933, 1934, and 1935. Yet, in 1935, Switzerland, represented by the " [:a {:href "/affiliations/5120"} "Zurich Rowing Club"] ", had won the Cup, which was only the second time since 1873 that Great Britain had been defeated in the tournament. By repeating their victory in 1936, the Swiss delegation – " [:a {:href "/athletes/39694"} "Hermann Betschart"] ", " [:a {:href "/athletes/39735"} "Alex"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/39737"} "Hans Homberger"] ", and " [:a {:href "/athletes/39796"} "Karl Schmid"] " – made themselves the first non-British favorites at the Olympics. They had also been the winners at the 1935 European Championships."] "\n\n" [:p {} "In the opening round, Switzerland defeated Great Britain in their heat by over three seconds, but the fastest time went to the Germans, represented by " [:a {:href "/athletes/37788"} "Rudi Eckstein"] ", " [:a {:href "/athletes/37871"} "Martin Karl"] ", " [:a {:href "/athletes/37925"} "Willi Menne"] ", and " [:a {:href "/athletes/37973"} "Toni Rom"] ", of " [:a {:href "/affiliations/5216"} "Würzburger RV Bayern"] ", who had won the 1934 European Championships. With the two victors advancing directly to the finals, the semi-finals essentially served as a repêchage, with Austria, Denmark (who had won the 1933 European Championships with a different crew), Great Britain, and Italy qualifying."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The Swiss rowers were hampered by exhaustion, its quartet having competed earlier in the " [:a {:href "/results/157975"} "coxed fours event"] " (they were also members of the " [:a {:href "/results/157985"} "coxed eights"] " crew), which led to the final evolving into a contest between Germany and Great Britain. The latter put out a strong effort, but the host nation won by a comfortable margin of nearly five seconds and took the gold medal. Great Britain settled for silver, while Switzerland managed to outperform the remaining nations and capture bronze."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitors"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} "Germany"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GER"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GER.png"}] "GER"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {}] [:td {:colspan "8"} [:a {:href "/athletes/37788"} "Rudi Eckstein"] " • " [:a {:href "/athletes/37973"} "Toni Rom"] " • " [:a {:href "/athletes/37871"} "Martin Karl"] " • " [:a {:href "/athletes/37925"} "Willi Menne"]]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} "Great Britain"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GBR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GBR.png"}] "GBR"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {}] [:td {:colspan "8"} [:a {:href "/athletes/37232"} "Martin Bristow"] " • " [:a {:href "/athletes/37207"} "Alan Barrett"] " • " [:a {:href "/athletes/37337"} "Peter Jackson"] " • " [:a {:href "/athletes/37481"} "Jan Sturrock"] " • " [:a {:href "/athletes/37378"} "McAllister Lonnon